# =============================================================================
# Lv-00 Axiom Package: Intuitionistic Propositional Logic (IPL)
# =============================================================================
#
# Corresponding Mathematical Theory:
#   Intuitionistic propositional logic (IPL), the formal system introduced by
#   Arend Heyting (1930) as a formalization of L.E.J. Brouwer's intuitionism.
#   IPL differs from classical propositional logic (CPL) by rejecting the law
#   of excluded middle (LEM) and double negation elimination (DNE), while
#   retaining ex falso quodlibet (EFQ).
#
#   This package covers the propositional fragment only (no quantifiers),
#   with →, ∧, ∨, ⊥ as primitive connectives and ¬ defined as φ → ⊥.
#
# Acknowledgement of distinction from the "constructive" contradiction_behavior
# value: Although Lv-00's design document associates "constructive" (blocking)
# with intuitionism, Heyting's formalization explicitly includes ⊥ → φ
# as an axiom schema. Therefore, this package uses "explosion_principle"
# for contradiction_behavior, reflecting the actual axiomatic content.
# The constructivity of IPL manifests in the absence of LEM and DNE,
# not in the absence of explosion.
#
# References:
#   - Heyting, A. (1930). "Die formalen Regeln der intuitionistischen Logik."
#     Sitzungsberichte der Preussischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 42-56.
#   - Wikipedia: Intuitionistic logic
#     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intuitionistic_logic
#   - Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy: Intuitionistic Logic
#     https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-intuitionistic/
#   - nLab: Intuitionistic logic
#     https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic
#   - Troelstra, A.S. & van Dalen, D. (1988). "Constructivism in Mathematics:
#     An Introduction." Vol. 1 & 2. North-Holland.
#   - Dummett, M. (2000). "Elements of Intuitionism" (2nd ed.). Oxford.
#   - Statman, R. (1979). "Intuitionistic propositional logic is polynomial-
#     space complete." Theoretical Computer Science, 9(1): 67-72.
#     https://doi.org/10.1016/0304-3975(79)90006-9
#   - Rybakov, V.V. (1997). "Admissibility of Logical Inference Rules."
#     Elsevier. (Admissibility in IPL is undecidable)
#
# Hilbert-style Axiomatization (Heyting, 1930):
#   Primitive connectives: →, ∧, ∨, ⊥
#   Defined: ¬φ ≡ φ → ⊥
#   Inference rule: Modus Ponens (MP): from φ and φ → ψ, infer ψ
#
#   Axiom Schemata (9):
#     THEN-1 (K):   φ → (ψ → φ)
#     THEN-2 (S):   (φ → (ψ → χ)) → ((φ → ψ) → (φ → χ))
#     AND-1:         φ ∧ ψ → φ
#     AND-2:         φ ∧ ψ → ψ
#     AND-3:         φ → (ψ → (φ ∧ ψ))
#     OR-1:          φ → φ ∨ ψ
#     OR-2:          ψ → φ ∨ ψ
#     OR-3:          (φ → χ) → ((ψ → χ) → (φ ∨ ψ → χ))
#     FALSE (EFQ):   ⊥ → φ
#
#   Classical theorems NOT valid in IPL:
#     - Law of Excluded Middle:       φ ∨ ¬φ
#     - Double Negation Elimination:  ¬¬φ → φ
#     - Peirce's Law:                 ((φ → ψ) → φ) → φ
#     - Full de Morgan for ∧:        ¬(φ ∧ ψ) → (¬φ ∨ ¬ψ)
#     - Implication as disjunction:  (φ → ψ) → (¬φ ∨ ψ)
#     - Dummett's axiom:             (φ → ψ) ∨ (ψ → φ)
#     - Classical reductio:          (¬φ → ⊥) → φ
#
#   Theorems valid in IPL but not obvious:
#     - Disjunctive Syllogism:       (φ ∨ ψ) ∧ ¬φ → ψ
#     - Double Negation Intro:       φ → ¬¬φ
#     - Triple Negation:             ¬¬¬φ → ¬φ
#     - Forward Contrapositive:      (φ → ψ) → (¬ψ → ¬φ)
#     - Glivenko's Theorem:          ¬¬φ is IPL-provable iff φ is CPL-provable
#     - Gödel-Gentzen Translation:   φ^N is IPL-provable iff φ is CPL-provable
#
# Key Meta-properties:
#   - Disjunction Property:  if ⊢ φ ∨ ψ, then ⊢ φ or ⊢ ψ
#   - Existence Property:    if ⊢ ∃x φ(x), then ⊢ φ(t) for some term t
#   - Decidability:          IPL is decidable (PSPACE-complete)
#   - Interpolation:         Craig interpolation holds
#
# Relationship to classical_propositional_logic (v1.0.0):
#   - IPL ⊂ CPL: every IPL theorem is a CPL theorem
#   - IPL + LEM = CPL (adding excluded middle recovers classical logic)
#   - IPL + DNE = CPL (adding double negation elimination is equivalent)
#   - IPL + Peirce's Law = CPL
#   - IPL is the logic of Heyting algebras (CPL = logic of Boolean algebras)
#   - Gödel-Gentzen negative translation: double negation embedding of CPL
#     into IPL, preserving provability
#
# Relationship to boolean_algebra (v1.0.0):
#   - IPL corresponds to Heyting algebras (not Boolean algebras)
#   - Every Boolean algebra is a Heyting algebra, but not conversely
#   - The Lindenbaum-Tarski algebra of IPL is the free Heyting algebra
#
# Complexity:
#   - IPL tautology checking: PSPACE-complete (Statman, 1979)
#   - IPL equivalence checking: PSPACE-complete
#   - IPL unification: undecidable
#   - Admissibility in IPL: undecidable (Rybakov, 1997)
#
# Author: Lv-00 Axiom Package System
# Date: 2026-05-21
# Version: 1.0.0
# =============================================================================

axiom "intuitionistic_propositional_logic" "1.0.0" {

    # =================================================================
    # Group I: Hilbert-style Core Axiom Schemata (9)
    # =================================================================
    # These are the axioms of Heyting's 1930 formalization.
    # Note: THEN-1 and THEN-2 are identical to axioms L1 (K) and L2 (S)
    # in Łukasiewicz's P2 system for classical logic.
    # The distinguishing feature is the ABSENCE of L3 (contrapositive
    # axiom (¬φ → ¬ψ) → (ψ → φ)), which is equivalent to DNE/LEM.
    # =================================================================

    # THEN-1 (K combinator): φ → (ψ → φ)
    # The principle of weakening / constant function
    # Params: proposition φ, proposition ψ
    template "axiom_K_weakening" 2

    # THEN-2 (S combinator): (φ → (ψ → χ)) → ((φ → ψ) → (φ → χ))
    # The principle of distribution / Frege's axiom
    # Params: proposition φ, proposition ψ, proposition χ
    template "axiom_S_distribution" 3

    # AND-1: φ ∧ ψ → φ
    # Conjunction left elimination
    # Params: proposition φ, proposition ψ
    template "conjunction_left_elim" 2

    # AND-2: φ ∧ ψ → ψ
    # Conjunction right elimination
    # Params: proposition φ, proposition ψ
    template "conjunction_right_elim" 2

    # AND-3: φ → (ψ → (φ ∧ ψ))
    # Conjunction introduction (axiom form)
    # Params: proposition φ, proposition ψ
    template "conjunction_intro_axiom" 2

    # OR-1: φ → φ ∨ ψ
    # Disjunction left introduction
    # Params: proposition φ, proposition ψ
    template "disjunction_left_intro" 2

    # OR-2: ψ → φ ∨ ψ
    # Disjunction right introduction
    # Params: proposition φ, proposition ψ
    template "disjunction_right_intro" 2

    # OR-3: (φ → χ) → ((ψ → χ) → (φ ∨ ψ → χ))
    # Disjunction elimination / proof by cases
    # Params: proposition φ, proposition ψ, proposition χ
    template "disjunction_elim" 3

    # FALSE (Ex Falso Quodlibet / Principle of Explosion): ⊥ → φ
    # From absurdity, anything follows.
    # NOTE: This IS included in Heyting's formalization, making IPL
    # an "explosive" constructive logic (unlike minimal logic).
    # Params: proposition φ
    template "ex_falso_quodlibet" 1

    # =================================================================
    # Group II: Inference Rules (1)
    # =================================================================

    # Modus Ponens (MP): from φ and φ → ψ, infer ψ
    # The sole rule of inference in the Hilbert-style system
    # Params: proposition φ, proposition φ → ψ
    template "modus_ponens" 2

    # =================================================================
    # Group III: Core Constructors — Primitive Connectives (5)
    # =================================================================

    # Implication (→): construct φ → ψ
    # The central connective of IPL; carries BHK constructive meaning
    # Params: proposition φ, proposition ψ
    template "implication" 2

    # Conjunction (∧): construct φ ∧ ψ
    # Params: proposition φ, proposition ψ
    template "conjunction" 2

    # Disjunction (∨): construct φ ∨ ψ
    # Carries the disjunction property: proof of φ ∨ ψ requires
    # proof of φ or proof of ψ
    # Params: proposition φ, proposition ψ
    template "disjunction" 2

    # Falsum (⊥): the absurd proposition / contradiction
    # The canonical "impossible" geometric object
    # Params: none
    template "falsum" 0

    # Negation (¬): defined as φ → ⊥
    # ¬φ means "φ implies a contradiction"
    # This is the standard intuitionistic definition
    # Params: proposition φ
    template "negation" 1

    # =================================================================
    # Group IV: Derived Connectives (5)
    # =================================================================

    # Biconditional (↔): φ ↔ ψ ≡ (φ → ψ) ∧ (ψ → φ)
    # Params: proposition φ, proposition ψ
    template "biconditional" 2

    # Exclusive or (⊕): φ ⊕ ψ ≡ (φ ∨ ψ) ∧ ¬(φ ∧ ψ)
    # Params: proposition φ, proposition ψ
    template "exclusive_or" 2

    # Sheffer stroke (NAND, |): φ | ψ ≡ ¬(φ ∧ ψ)
    # Functionally complete for IPL (but NOT for CPL without LEM)
    # Params: proposition φ, proposition ψ
    template "sheffer_stroke" 2

    # Peirce arrow (NOR, ↓): φ ↓ ψ ≡ ¬(φ ∨ ψ)
    # Params: proposition φ, proposition ψ
    template "peirce_arrow" 2

    # Verum (⊤): the trivially true proposition ≡ ⊥ → ⊥
    # Params: none
    template "verum" 0

    # =================================================================
    # Group V: Derived Inference Rules — ALL IPL-VALID (14)
    # =================================================================
    # IMPORTANT: All rules below are intuitionistically valid.
    # Rules requiring LEM or DNE (like classical contrapositive
    # (¬φ → ¬ψ) → (ψ → φ)) are NOT included here.
    # =================================================================

    # Hypothetical syllogism: from φ → ψ and ψ → χ, infer φ → χ
    # Params: φ → ψ, ψ → χ
    template "hypothetical_syllogism" 3

    # Modus tollens: from φ → ψ and ¬ψ, infer ¬φ
    # NOTE: This IS intuitionistically valid! Proof:
    #   Assume φ → ψ and ¬ψ (i.e., ψ → ⊥). Assume φ. Then ψ (MP). Then ⊥.
    #   So φ → ⊥, i.e., ¬φ.
    # Params: φ → ψ, ¬ψ
    template "modus_tollens" 2

    # Disjunctive syllogism: from φ ∨ ψ and ¬φ, infer ψ
    # NOTE: This IS intuitionistically valid! Proof:
    #   Case φ: from ¬φ (φ → ⊥), get ⊥, then ψ by EFQ.
    #   Case ψ: trivial.
    #   By OR-3, φ ∨ ψ → ψ. With φ ∨ ψ, conclude ψ.
    # Params: φ ∨ ψ, ¬φ
    template "disjunctive_syllogism" 2

    # Conjunction introduction: from φ and ψ, infer φ ∧ ψ
    # Params: φ, ψ
    template "conjunction_introduction" 2

    # Conjunction elimination (left): from φ ∧ ψ, infer φ
    # Params: φ ∧ ψ
    template "conjunction_elimination_left" 1

    # Conjunction elimination (right): from φ ∧ ψ, infer ψ
    # Params: φ ∧ ψ
    template "conjunction_elimination_right" 1

    # Disjunction introduction (left): from φ, infer φ ∨ ψ
    # Params: φ, ψ (target)
    template "disjunction_introduction_left" 2

    # Disjunction introduction (right): from ψ, infer φ ∨ ψ
    # Params: φ (target), ψ
    template "disjunction_introduction_right" 2

    # Biconditional introduction: from φ → ψ and ψ → φ, infer φ ↔ ψ
    # Params: φ → ψ, ψ → φ
    template "biconditional_introduction" 2

    # Biconditional elimination (left): from φ ↔ ψ, infer φ → ψ
    # Params: φ ↔ ψ
    template "biconditional_elimination_left" 1

    # Biconditional elimination (right): from φ ↔ ψ, infer ψ → φ
    # Params: φ ↔ ψ
    template "biconditional_elimination_right" 1

    # Double negation introduction: from φ, infer ¬¬φ
    # VALID in IPL: φ → ((φ → ⊥) → ⊥)
    # This IS provable from THEN-1, THEN-2, and modus ponens.
    # Note: The converse (¬¬φ → φ) is NOT intuitionistically valid.
    # Params: φ
    template "double_negation_introduction" 1

    # Reductio ad absurdum (intuitionistic): from φ → ⊥, infer ¬φ
    # This is essentially the definition of ¬, so it's trivially valid.
    # Distinguished from classical reductio (¬φ → ⊥ ⊢ φ) which is DNE.
    # Params: φ, ⊥
    template "reductio_ad_absurdum" 2

    # Deduction theorem: if Γ, φ ⊢ ψ, then Γ ⊢ φ → ψ
    # This is a meta-theorem of IPL (and Hilbert systems generally).
    # Params: φ (assumption), ψ (conclusion)
    template "deduction_theorem" 2

    # =================================================================
    # Group VI: Propositional Identities — ALL IPL-VALID (8)
    # =================================================================

    # De Morgan for disjunction (full): ¬(φ ∨ ψ) ↔ (¬φ ∧ ¬ψ)
    # Both directions are intuitionistically valid.
    # Params: φ, ψ
    template "de_morgan_disjunction" 2

    # De Morgan for conjunction (weakened): (¬φ ∨ ¬ψ) → ¬(φ ∧ ψ)
    # Only this direction is intuitionistically valid.
    # The converse ¬(φ ∧ ψ) → (¬φ ∨ ¬ψ) requires LEM and is NOT valid.
    # Params: φ, ψ
    template "de_morgan_conjunction_weakened" 2

    # Conjunction idempotence: φ ∧ φ ↔ φ
    # Params: φ
    template "conjunction_idempotence" 1

    # Disjunction idempotence: φ ∨ φ ↔ φ
    # Params: φ
    template "disjunction_idempotence" 1

    # Conjunction commutativity: φ ∧ ψ ↔ ψ ∧ φ
    # Params: φ, ψ
    template "conjunction_commutativity" 2

    # Disjunction commutativity: φ ∨ ψ ↔ ψ ∨ φ
    # Params: φ, ψ
    template "disjunction_commutativity" 2

    # Conjunction associativity: (φ ∧ ψ) ∧ χ ↔ φ ∧ (ψ ∧ χ)
    # Params: φ, ψ, χ
    template "conjunction_associativity" 3

    # Disjunction associativity: (φ ∨ ψ) ∨ χ ↔ φ ∨ (ψ ∨ χ)
    # Params: φ, ψ, χ
    template "disjunction_associativity" 3

    # =================================================================
    # Group VII: IPL-CPL Bridge Theorems (4)
    # =================================================================
    # These theorems characterize the precise relationship between
    # intuitionistic and classical propositional logic.
    # =================================================================

    # Glivenko's Theorem (1929): φ is a CPL tautology iff ¬¬φ is an IPL theorem.
    # Provides a double-negation embedding of CPL into IPL.
    # Params: φ (any proposition)
    template "glivenko_double_negation" 1

    # Gödel-Gentzen Negative Translation (1933):
    # A compositional translation (·)^N such that φ is CPL-provable
    # iff φ^N is IPL-provable.
    # Key clauses: (φ → ψ)^N = φ^N → ψ^N
    #              (¬φ)^N = ¬(φ^N)
    #              P^N = ¬¬P (for atomic P)
    #              (φ ∨ ψ)^N = ¬(¬φ^N ∧ ¬ψ^N) = ¬¬(φ^N ∨ ψ^N)
    # Params: φ (source), φ^N (translated)
    template "negative_translation" 2

    # Gödel-Gentzen translation preserves derivability
    # If ⊢_CPL φ, then ⊢_IPL φ^N
    # This is the soundness direction of the negative translation.
    # Params: φ (classical proof), φ^N (intuitionistic translation)
    template "negative_translation_soundness" 2

    # Kuroda's Negative Translation (1951):
    # Alternative translation: φ^K = ¬¬φ^* where (·)^* commutes with all
    # connectives except ∨:
    #   (∀x φ)^* = ∀x ¬¬(φ^*)
    # For propositional fragment, this simplifies.
    # Params: φ (source), φ^K (translated)
    template "kuroda_translation" 2

    # =================================================================
    # Group VIII: Forward Contrapositive & Derived Rules (5)
    # =================================================================

    # Forward contrapositive: (φ → ψ) → (¬ψ → ¬φ)
    # This IS intuitionistically valid (unlike the full classical
    # contrapositive (¬ψ → ¬φ) → (φ → ψ)).
    # Params: φ → ψ
    template "contraposition_forward" 2

    # Exportation: (φ ∧ ψ → χ) → (φ → (ψ → χ))
    # Valid in IPL (uses AND-1/2/3 and THEN-1/2)
    # Params: φ, ψ, χ
    template "exportation" 3

    # Importation: (φ → (ψ → χ)) → (φ ∧ ψ → χ)
    # Valid in IPL (converse of exportation)
    # Params: φ, ψ, χ
    template "importation" 3

    # Proof by cases (intuitionistic): from φ ∨ ψ, φ → χ, ψ → χ, infer χ
    # This is a direct application of OR-3.
    # Params: φ ∨ ψ, φ → χ, ψ → χ
    template "proof_by_cases" 3

    # Triple negation reduction: ¬¬¬φ → ¬φ
    # Valid in IPL (a corollary of double negation introduction)
    # Params: ¬¬¬φ
    template "triple_negation_reduction" 1

    # =================================================================
    # Group IX: NOT Included — Classical-Only Theorems (explicitly listed)
    # =================================================================
    # These are explicitly NOT included as templates because they are
    # NOT intuitionistically valid. They are listed here for reference
    # and to contrast with the classical_propositional_logic package.
    #
    # NOT INCLUDED: law_of_excluded_middle (φ ∨ ¬φ)
    # NOT INCLUDED: double_negation_elimination (¬¬φ → φ)
    # NOT INCLUDED: peirces_law (((φ → ψ) → φ) → φ)
    # NOT INCLUDED: classical_contrapositive ((¬ψ → ¬φ) → (φ → ψ))
    # NOT INCLUDED: implication_as_disjunction ((φ → ψ) → (¬φ ∨ ψ))
    # NOT INCLUDED: dummett_axiom ((φ → ψ) ∨ (ψ → φ))
    # NOT INCLUDED: classical_reductio ((¬φ → ⊥) → φ)
    # NOT INCLUDED: de_morgan_conjunction_full (¬(φ ∧ ψ) → (¬φ ∨ ¬ψ))
    # NOT INCLUDED: forall_not_not (¬¬∀x φ → ∀x ¬¬φ)
    # =================================================================

    # =================================================================
    # Known Unconstructible / Undecidable Problems (6)
    # =================================================================

    unconstructible "intuitionistic_tautology_checking" {
        reduces_to "PSPACE_complete"
        dependency "implication"
        dependency "conjunction"
        dependency "disjunction"
        dependency "negation"
        dependency "falsum"
        dependency "axiom_K_weakening"
        dependency "axiom_S_distribution"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intuitionistic_logic"
        green_verified true
    }

    unconstructible "intuitionistic_satisfiability" {
        reduces_to "PSPACE_complete"
        dependency "implication"
        dependency "conjunction"
        dependency "disjunction"
        dependency "negation"
        dependency "falsum"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intuitionistic_logic"
        green_verified true
    }

    unconstructible "intuitionistic_equivalence_checking" {
        reduces_to "PSPACE_complete"
        dependency "implication"
        dependency "conjunction"
        dependency "disjunction"
        dependency "negation"
        dependency "biconditional"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intuitionistic_logic"
        green_verified true
    }

    unconstructible "intuitionistic_unification_problem" {
        reduces_to "undecidable"
        dependency "implication"
        dependency "conjunction"
        dependency "disjunction"
        dependency "negation"
        dependency "falsum"
        dependency "modus_ponens"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unification_(computer_science)"
        green_verified true
    }

    unconstructible "admissibility_in_IPL" {
        reduces_to "undecidable"
        dependency "implication"
        dependency "modus_ponens"
        dependency "axiom_K_weakening"
        dependency "axiom_S_distribution"
        dependency "hypothetical_syllogism"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Admissible_rule"
        green_verified true
    }

    unconstructible "intermediate_logic_axiomatization" {
        reduces_to "undecidable"
        dependency "implication"
        dependency "conjunction"
        dependency "disjunction"
        dependency "negation"
        dependency "falsum"
        dependency "modus_ponens"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intermediate_logic"
        green_verified true
    }

    # =================================================================
    # Bottom Geometry & Logical Framework
    # =================================================================
    #
    # bottom_geometry: intuitionistic_propositional_heyting
    #   ⊥ represents an "uninhabited" geometric pattern — a place where
    #   no constructive witness can exist. Unlike the Boolean ⊥ which is
    #   simply "false" among two truth values, the intuitionistic ⊥ is
    #   the pattern of "provable absurdity" — a geometric dead end that
    #   prevents constructive completion but still permits explosion
    #   derivation via EFQ.
    #
    # negation_encoding: implication_to_falsum
    #   ¬φ ≡ φ → ⊥ — the standard intuitionistic definition.
    #   Under BHK: ¬φ means "any proof of φ can be transformed into
    #   a proof of ⊥ (which is impossible)". This is purely negative
    #   (unlike the Boolean complement which pairs with ∨ to give LEM).
    #
    # contradiction_behavior: explosion_principle
    #   Although Lv-00's design intent associates "constructive"/blocking
    #   with intuitionism, Heyting's 1930 formalization explicitly includes
    #   ⊥ → φ as axiom schema FALSE. Therefore, from contradiction, anything
    #   follows in IPL. The constructivity is enforced by the absence of
    #   LEM and DNE, not by blocking explosion.
    # =================================================================

    bottom_geometry "intuitionistic_propositional_heyting"
    negation_encoding "implication_to_falsum"
    contradiction_behavior "explosion_principle"
}
